<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1916</span> · Also known as Dadaism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dada was an avant-garde art movement founded in 1916 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cabaret-voltaire">Cabaret Voltaire</a> in Zurich by artists who had taken refuge in neutral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Rejecting reason and bourgeois art, which they blamed for the war, its members, among them Hugo Ball, Tristan Tzara and Hans Arp, used nonsense, chance and collage. It spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and prepared the way for Surrealism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dada.jpg" alt="Dada" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grand_opening_of_the_first_Dada_exhibition,_Berlin,_5_June_1920.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
